---
// The shell every page of both editions is served in.
//
// What this replaces: the head block was written out by hand on all 44 pages,
// eighteen tags each, and SEVEN of those tags carry an absolute URL. That is
// 176 absolute URLs maintained by copying, and a wrong one is invisible: a
// canonical pointing at the wrong page still renders, still validates, and
// quietly tells a search engine that this page is a duplicate of another. Here
// they are computed once, from `url`, and cannot disagree with each other.
//
// EVERY URL ENDS IN A SLASH, and that is the host's rule rather than a taste.
// Measured live against this project's own Cloudflare Pages deployment on
// 2026-08-28: `/why.html` answers **308** and so does `/docs`, while `/why/`
// and `/docs/` answer 200. Pages strips the extension whether you want it to or
// not, so a site that advertises `.html` advertises URLs that redirect, and a
// canonical pointing at a redirect is a page arguing with itself. The site now
// spells its URLs the way its host serves them, which is also the spelling the
// author's other site uses: `trailingSlash: 'always'`.
//
// The `<footer>` IS here, through `SiteFooter`, and the paragraph this one
// replaces said why it was not: six distinct footers among eleven pages, and
// pulling them into a component meant deciding by eye which sentences were
// furniture and which were content. The build comparison that paragraph asked
// for was run on 2026-09-08, over all 180 pages, and it settled the question
// the other way: every footer is the page's own paragraphs, then the byline,
// then the copyright line, so the cut falls on an element boundary and needs
// no eye. `splitFooter` makes it: the page's `<footer>` is lifted out of the
// rendered slot and its paragraphs go into the site footer above the shared
// tail, which is why no wrapper under `pages/` had to change.
//
// In the source these pages come from, `<footer>` sits INSIDE `<main>`. The CSS
// is written against that (`main > footer`), so the component renders inside
// `<main>` too.
//
// `noindex` is the two 404 pages and nothing else. Cloudflare Pages serves a
// not-found path with a 404 status, which already tells a crawler everything,
// but the file also sits at an address of its own, `/404/` and `/it/404/`,
// where it would answer 200 and be indexable like any page. `follow` stays on,
// because the four links out of that page are the only useful thing about it.
// The sitemap keeps both out separately, in `astro.config.mjs`: one guard for
// what the site advertises, one for a crawler that arrives anyway.

import SiteNav from '../components/SiteNav.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import { splitFooter } from '../lib/footer.ts';

interface Props {
  /** The URL this page answers at, absolute, ending in `/`. The one input. */
  url: string;
  title: string;
  description: string;
  /** Which nav entry to mark as current. Omitted on the two home pages. */
  current?: string;
  /** `home` on the two landings, absent everywhere else. */
  mainClass?: string;
  /** The two 404 pages, and nothing else: see the robots tag below. */
  noindex?: boolean;
}

const { url, title, description, current, mainClass, noindex } = Astro.props;

// The page, rendered, then cut once: its own closing paragraphs go to the
// footer and everything else is the body.
const { body, closing } = splitFooter(await Astro.slots.render('default'), url);

const ORIGIN = 'https://heroes-lang.org';

const lang = url.startsWith('/it/') || url === '/it/' ? 'it' : 'en';

// The English twin of this page, and its Italian one. Mirrored paths are what
// make this two lines; `x-default` points at the English, as it did before.
const en = lang === 'it' ? url.slice(3) : url;
const it = `/it${en}`;
const canonical = ORIGIN + url;

// SELF FIRST, then the other edition, then x-default. Measured across the site
// this replaces: all 22 English pages listed `en, it, x-default` and all 22
// Italian ones listed `it, en, x-default`, without a single exception. The
// order carries no meaning to a crawler, and reproducing it costs one line;
// spending that line is what lets the build comparison report a real difference
// instead of forty-four false ones.
const alternates = lang === 'it'
  ? [['it', it], ['en', en], ['x-default', en]]
  : [['en', en], ['it', it], ['x-default', en]];

// The icon is three FILES under `public/`, and it used to be a `data:` URI
// holding the bolt inline. That was smaller and it was unreachable: a `data:`
// URI is not an address, so a crawler cannot fetch it, and Google shows the
// icon beside a result on a phone by fetching one. Measured before this
// changed: `https://heroes-lang.org/favicon.ico` answered 404, which is the
// address a client tries when the markup gives it nothing it can use.
//
// `favicon.svg` is the source the other two are rendered from, so the bolt is
// drawn once in the tree rather than once here and once in a file that could
// drift from it. `apple-touch-icon.png` carries the site's own near-black
// ground because iOS drops transparency and rounds the corners itself.

// -- What a machine that is not a browser gets ------------------------------
//
// Everything below is computed from `url` for the same reason the canonical and
// the hreflangs are: a card, a breadcrumb and a canonical that disagree are
// worse than none, and the only way three derived facts cannot disagree is if
// one expression derives all three. No page passes any of it in.
//
// The author asked for this, 2026-09-07, in as many words: all the SEO tags,
// and push them hard.

const AUTHOR = 'Giuseppe Arici';

// The share card, per SECTION and per edition, which is twenty of them.
//
// Two site-wide cards were built first and they were the wrong shape for the
// job: a link posted into a channel is a link to ONE page, and a card that says
// only the name of the site tells the reader nothing about the page they were
// sent. The section is the level that pays, because it is the level a card can
// still describe honestly in one line. The 134 example pages all get the
// examples card and the 30 chapters all get the docs card, which is right:
// `examples/nqueens/` and `examples/sieve/` really are the same promise.
//
// 1200x630 is the size every network crops from. All twenty are rendered from
// the site's own tokens and the site's own h1 face, so the card and the page
// agree about what the wordmark looks like; the pass before this one set it in
// a serif and the two disagreed. None carries a number, because a measured
// number inside an image cannot be checked by whoever reads it (CLAUDE.md §11),
// which is why the examples card does not say how many programs there are.
//
// The fallback is the home card rather than nothing, so a page in a section
// nobody has drawn yet still shares as this site rather than as a bare link.
//
// `errors` was in this set and was unreachable. The errors page became chapter
// two of the guide, so its address is `/docs/errors/`, and `section` reads the
// FIRST path segment, which is `docs`. The two `errors-*.png` were rendered,
// tracked, and asked for by no page at all. The chapter takes the docs card,
// which is right, and the set now names only sections that exist.
const SECTION_CARDS = new Set([
  'home', 'why', 'start', 'docs', 'spec', 'examples', 'log', 'project', 'about',
]);
const section = (() => {
  const path = lang === 'it' ? url.slice(3) : url;
  const first = path.split('/').filter(Boolean)[0];
  return first !== undefined && SECTION_CARDS.has(first) ? first : 'home';
})();
const card = `/images/card/${section}-${lang}.png`;
// Both alts are short, and the Italian one is short for a REASON rather than
// for brevity: `records/english` reads every living file outside
// `src/html/it/` and fails a line carrying six or more Italian words, because
// Italian prose belongs to the edition and not to a shared layout. It caught
// this alt on its first run. The card's own words are in the image; the alt
// names what the image is.
const cardAlt = lang === 'it'
  ? 'Heroes, un linguaggio compilato'
  : 'Heroes, a compiled programming language';

const locale = lang === 'it' ? 'it_IT' : 'en_US';
const localeAlt = lang === 'it' ? 'en_US' : 'it_IT';

// The breadcrumb a search result shows instead of the raw URL. Derived from the
// path, so a page that moves takes its trail with it and a page nobody thought
// about still has one. `/it/` is the Italian ROOT and not a crumb of its own,
// which is why the segments are taken after it.
const segments = (lang === 'it' ? url.slice(3) : url).split('/').filter(Boolean);
const root = lang === 'it' ? '/it/' : '/';
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Heroes', item: ORIGIN + root }];
let walked = root;
for (const segment of segments) {
  walked += `${segment}/`;
  // Every crumb is its own slug with the hyphens opened out, the last one
  // included. The page's TITLE was tried there first and read worse in the one
  // place a breadcrumb is ever seen: a search result rendered
  // "Heroes / examples / examples/json/" and "Heroes / docs / Maps, Heroes
  // documentation", because a title is written to stand alone and a crumb
  // never does. The slug is already the short name, and it cannot repeat the
  // crumb above it.
  crumbs.push({ name: segment.replaceAll('-', ' '), item: ORIGIN + walked });
}

// What KIND of page this is, in the vocabulary a crawler already has. A site
// whose pages are all `WebPage` has told a machine nothing it could not see.
const kind = (() => {
  const path = lang === 'it' ? url.slice(3) : url;
  if (path === '/') return 'WebSite';
  if (path.startsWith('/examples/')) return 'SoftwareSourceCode';
  if (path.startsWith('/docs/') || path === '/spec/') return 'TechArticle';
  if (path.startsWith('/about')) return 'AboutPage';
  return 'WebPage';
})();

const publisher = {
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: AUTHOR,
  url: 'https://giuseppearici.com/',
};

const page: Record<string, unknown> = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': kind,
  name: title,
  description,
  url: canonical,
  inLanguage: lang,
  image: ORIGIN + card,
  isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'Heroes', url: ORIGIN + root },
  author: publisher,
  publisher,
};
if (kind === 'SoftwareSourceCode') {
  page.programmingLanguage = { '@type': 'ComputerLanguage', name: 'Heroes' };
  page.codeSampleType = 'full';
}
if (kind === 'WebSite') delete page.isPartOf;
// `headline` is an article's property and schema.org's own validator says so.
if (kind === 'TechArticle' || kind === 'SoftwareSourceCode') page.headline = title;

const breadcrumbs = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: crumbs.map((crumb, index) => ({
    '@type': 'ListItem',
    position: index + 1,
    name: crumb.name,
    item: crumb.item,
  })),
};

// The language itself, once, on the two landings. `ComputerLanguage` is the
// type schema.org has for exactly this and almost nobody uses; the site is a
// language's site, so it is the one type that describes the subject rather
// than the document.
const language = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'ComputerLanguage',
  name: 'Heroes',
  alternateName: 'Heroes language',
  url: ORIGIN + root,
  description,
  author: publisher,
  runtimePlatform: 'C11, compiled by clang',
  // Read off `LICENSE` in the repository root rather than assumed. It was typed
  // here as MIT first and the file says Apache 2.0, with a second file,
  // `LICENSE-RUNTIME-EXCEPTION`, beside it. A licence a machine reads is a
  // claim like any other.
  license: 'https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0',
};

// `og:type` follows the same decision. It was `website` on all 180 pages,
// which tells a card renderer that a chapter and the front page are the same
// kind of thing; `article` is what the eighty pages of prose actually are.
//
// The line under this comment used to send `WebPage` to `website` as well,
// which left the comment describing something the code did not do: `/why/`,
// `/start/`, `/log/` and `/project/`, four pages of prose per edition and eight
// in all, went on declaring themselves front pages. Only the two landings are
// the site rather than a piece of it, so only they are `website`.
const ogType = kind === 'WebSite' ? 'website' : 'article';

const structured = kind === 'WebSite' ? [page, breadcrumbs, language] : [page, breadcrumbs];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang={lang}>
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{title}</title>
<meta name="description" content={description}>
<link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="32x32">
<link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
<link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png">
<link rel="canonical" href={canonical}>
{alternates.map(([code, href]) => (
<link rel="alternate" hreflang={code} href={ORIGIN + href}>
))}
<meta name="theme-color" content="#0b0b10" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)">
<meta name="theme-color" content="#f7f2e6" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)">
<meta name="author" content={AUTHOR}>
<meta name="application-name" content="Heroes">
<meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-title" content="Heroes">
<meta name="color-scheme" content="dark light">
<!-- `index, follow` is the default, so it is written here for the three that
     are NOT: an unbounded snippet, a large image preview, and no video cap.
     Without them a crawler picks conservative defaults and the share card and
     the description are cropped by somebody else's guess. The `noindex` branch
     is the two 404 pages, and the block at the top of this file says why. -->
<meta name="robots" content={noindex
  ? 'noindex, follow'
  : 'index, follow, max-snippet:-1, max-image-preview:large, max-video-preview:-1'}>
<meta property="og:type" content={ogType}>
<meta property="og:site_name" content="Heroes">
<meta property="og:title" content={title}>
<meta property="og:description" content={description}>
<meta property="og:url" content={canonical}>
<meta property="og:locale" content={locale}>
<meta property="og:locale:alternate" content={localeAlt}>
<meta property="og:image" content={ORIGIN + card}>
<meta property="og:image:type" content="image/png">
<meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
<meta property="og:image:height" content="630">
<meta property="og:image:alt" content={cardAlt}>
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
<meta name="twitter:title" content={title}>
<meta name="twitter:description" content={description}>
<meta name="twitter:image" content={ORIGIN + card}>
<meta name="twitter:image:alt" content={cardAlt}>
<link rel="author" href="https://giuseppearici.com/">
<link rel="sitemap" type="application/xml" href="/sitemap-index.xml">
<link rel="alternate" type="application/atom+xml" title="Heroes: the build log" href="/log.xml">
<link rel="alternate" type="text/markdown" title="Heroes: the whole language in one file" href="/spec.md">
<link rel="preload" href="/style.css" as="style">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/style.css">
{structured.map((thing) => (
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(thing)}></script>
))}
</head>
<body>
<SiteNav url={url} current={current} />
<main class={mainClass}>
<Fragment set:html={body} />
<SiteFooter url={url}><Fragment set:html={closing} /></SiteFooter>
</main>
</body>
</html>
